  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Talking with friends",
     "texto": [
      "- To agree or close: **vale**, **venga**, **hecho**.\n- To deny: **qué va**, **para nada**, **ni hablar**.\n- Not to know or care: **ni idea**, **yo qué sé**, **me da igual**.\n- To react: **¡Anda!**, **¡No me digas!**, **¡Qué fuerte!**, **¡Qué rollo!**, **¡Qué guay!**\n- To address someone: **tío**, **tía**; and **hombre** or **mujer** for emphasis.\n- To say «a lot»: **un montón**, **mogollón**.\n\nColloquial verbs: **currar** (to work), **molar** (to like), **flipar** (to be amazed), **pillar** (to catch, to get).\n\nColloquial nouns: **la pasta** (money), **el curro** (the job).\n\nSpanish also shortens words: **el finde**, **la peli**, **el profe**, **el boli**, **el cole**.\n\nAll of this is for friends and family. In an interview, an email or with a stranger, use the neutral word."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Colloquial and neutral",
     "texto": [
      "Every colloquial word has a neutral one. Choose according to whom you are talking to.\n\n- **currar** → trabajar\n- **la pasta** → el dinero\n- **molar** → gustar\n- **flipar** → sorprenderse mucho\n- **un montón** → mucho\n- **el finde** → el fin de semana\n- **estar hecho polvo** → estar muy cansado\n\n**Molar** works like **gustar**: **me mola** this song, **nos molan** Sundays.\n\n**Register.** With friends and family, colloquial speech sounds natural. With a boss, in an email or in an interview, it sounds too informal."
     ],
